'''Tamura Shrine''' ({{ill|Tamura Jinja|de|Tamura‑jinja|ja|田村神社|zh|田村神社}}) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|de|Shinto‑Schrein|ja|神社|zh|神社}} located in Tsuchiyama Town, Kōka City, Shiga Prefecture, Japan. The shrine held the rank of a former prefectural shrine under the modern shrine ranking system. The {{ill|Tōkaidō|de|Tōkaidō|ja|東海道|zh|东海道}} passes through part of the approach from the first to the second torii and from the second torii to the {{ill|Kaido Bridge|de|Kaido‑Brücke|ja|海道橋|zh|海道桥}}.

Every year, the shrine is famous for its Great Exorcism Festival ({{ill|yakuyoke taisai|ja|厄除大祭}}) held over three days centered on {{ill|February 18|de|18. Februar|ja|2月18日|zh|2月18日}}.

== History ==
On April 8 in the 45th year of Emperor Suinin’s reign, {{ill|Kakao|de|Kakao|ja|甲可翁|zh|甲可翁}} enshrined the spirit of {{ill|Princess Yamatohime|ja|倭姫命}} at {{ill|Kaka Hiunomiya|de|Kaka Hiunomiya|ja|甲可日雲宮|zh|甲可日云宫}}, naming it {{ill|Kōza Daimyōjin|de|Kōza‑Daimyōjin|ja|高座大明神|zh|高座大明神}} – this is considered the origin of Tamura Shrine.

During the {{ill|Heian period|de|Heian‑Zeit|ja|平安時代|zh|平安时代}}, {{ill|Sakanoue no Tamuramaro|ja|坂上田村麻呂}} was ordered by {{ill|Emperor Saga|ja|嵯峨天皇}} to pacify the {{ill|evil spirits|de|Böse Geister|ja|悪鬼|zh|恶鬼}} of {{ill|Mount Suzuka|de|Suzuka‑Berg|ja|鈴鹿峠|zh|铃鹿峠}}. After swiftly subduing them, he shot an arrow and said, “By the merit of this arrow, may it ward off disaster for all people. Enshrine me where this arrow falls.” He thereafter had the main hall built at that spot.

In 812 (3rd year of Kōnin), by imperial decree of {{ill|Emperor Saga|ja|嵯峨天皇}}, a sanctuary for Sakanoue no Tamuramaro was established on the twin peaks of Suzuka Pass<ref name="Kirimura2012">{{harv|Kirimura|2012|page=121}}</ref>. That same year, when an epidemic occurred nearby, the emperor ordered a grand exorcism rite at this shrine, leading to its veneration as a god of exorcism and, because Tamuramaro cleared the pass of evil spirits ensuring safe passage, as a protector of travelers.

On April 8, 822 (13th year of Kōnin), the sanctuary for Tamuramaro from the twin peaks was moved beside the original Kōza Daimyōjin and renamed {{ill|Kōza Tamura Daimyōjin|de|Kōza Tamura‑Daimyōjin|ja|高座田村大明神|zh|高座田村大明神}}.

During the {{ill|Tenbun era|ja|天文|zh|天文}} wars, the shrine lost its Imperial plaque by Emperor Saga, but in 1638 (15th year of {{ill|Kan’ei|ja|寛永}}), it received a plaque inscribed {{ill|Shōichii Tamura Daimyōjin|ja|正一位田村大明神}} from {{ill|Emperor Go‑Mizunoo|ja|後水尾天皇}}, which was affixed to the torii gate<ref name="miejinja">{{Cite web|title=〔甲賀〕 田村神社|publisher=Shiga Shrine Office|url=http://www.shiga-jinjacho.jp/...|accessdate=2018-02-19}}</ref>. Throughout the Edo period, the Tamura clan, descendants of Tamuramaro and lords of the Ichinoseki Domain, continued to revere the shrine.

A temple, {{ill|Jingū-ji|ja|神宮寺}}, once stood as the shrine’s bettō temple, housing the thousand-armed Kannon statue created by {{ill|Enchin|ja|延鎮}} – Tamuramaro’s personal guardian Buddha – but in 1868 (Keiō 4), due to {{ill|Shinbutsu bunri|ja|神仏分離|zh|神佛分离}}, relations with the temple ended and the statue was moved to {{ill|Ei’un-ji|ja|永雲寺}}<ref>甲賀郡教育会『甲賀郡志 第3巻』(1926), 907頁</ref>.

In 1872 (Meiji 5), the shrine was renamed {{ill|Kōza Shrine|ja|高座神社}} and on April 6, 1887 (Meiji 20), officially renamed Tamura Shrine<ref name="#1"/> and designated a prefectural shrine.

In 1907 (Meiji 40), the Tamura Shrine on the twin peaks at Suzuka Pass was merged into {{ill|Katayama Shrine (Kameyama)|ja|片山神社 (亀山市)|zh|片山神社 (亀山市)}} in Kameyama, Mie Prefecture.

The shrine treasures include the imperial arrowhead “{{ill|Chobaya no Mikura|ja|楚葉矢の御剣|zh|楚叶矢之御剑}}” and three arrowheads<ref name="#1"/>.

== Deities ==
* Primary – {{ill|Sakanoue no Tamuramaro|ja|坂上田村麻呂}}, {{ill|Emperor Saga|ja|嵯峨天皇}}, {{ill|Princess Yamatohime|ja|倭姫命}}
* Accompanying – {{ill|Inazuramotono-mikoto|ja|稲倉魂命}}, {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大己貴命}}, {{ill|Kotoshironushi|ja|国狭槌尊}}, {{ill|Ōyamatsumi|ja|大山咋神}}, {{ill|Aramitama|ja|荒魂}}

